WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.086 --> 00:07.656 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference March 20,

00:07.936 --> 00:09.936 align:middle
2019 CHAIR POWELL.

00:09.936 --> 00:11.446 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone,
and welcome.

00:11.446 --> 00:13.656 align:middle
I will begin with an overview
of economic conditions

00:13.656 --> 00:14.256 align:middle
and an explanation

00:14.256 --> 00:16.726 align:middle
of the decisions the Committee
made at today's meeting.

00:16.726 --> 00:18.896 align:middle
My colleagues and I have
one overarching goal:

00:18.966 --> 00:20.816 align:middle
to sustain the economic
expansion,

00:21.626 --> 00:24.326 align:middle
with a strong job market and
stable prices, for the benefit

00:24.326 --> 00:25.166 align:middle
of the American people.

00:26.256 --> 00:28.976 align:middle
The U.S. economy is in a good
place, and we will continue

00:28.976 --> 00:31.706 align:middle
to use our monetary policy
tools to help keep it there.

00:33.156 --> 00:35.966 align:middle
The jobs market is strong,
showing healthier wage gains

00:35.966 --> 00:39.266 align:middle
and prompting many people to
join or remain in the workforce.

00:40.396 --> 00:42.816 align:middle
The unemployment rate
is near historic lows,

00:42.816 --> 00:45.056 align:middle
and inflation remains
near our 2 percent goal.

00:46.116 --> 00:48.746 align:middle
We continue to expect that
the American economy will grow

00:48.746 --> 00:52.466 align:middle
at a solid pace in 2019,
although likely slower

00:52.466 --> 00:54.716 align:middle
than the very strong
pace of 2018.

00:55.766 --> 00:58.516 align:middle
We believe that our current
policy stance is appropriate.

01:00.356 --> 01:03.036 align:middle
Since last year, however, we
have noted some developments

01:03.036 --> 01:05.526 align:middle
at home and around the world
that bear close attention.

01:06.846 --> 01:09.366 align:middle
Given the overall favorable
conditions in our economy,

01:09.366 --> 01:11.956 align:middle
my colleagues and I will be
patient in assessing what,

01:12.016 --> 01:14.966 align:middle
if any, changes in the stance
of policy may be needed.

01:16.026 --> 01:19.336 align:middle
Let me explain in more detail
how incoming data warrant our

01:19.336 --> 01:22.476 align:middle
current stance and a wait
and see approach to changes.

01:22.886 --> 01:26.756 align:middle
With the benefit of fiscal
stimulus and other tailwinds,

01:26.966 --> 01:30.956 align:middle
growth in 2018 was strong-in
fact, at 3.1 percent,

01:31.276 --> 01:33.136 align:middle
the strongest year in
more than a decade.

01:34.296 --> 01:36.796 align:middle
For some time, most forecasts
have called for growth

01:36.796 --> 01:40.156 align:middle
to continue in 2019
at a somewhat lower

01:40.676 --> 01:41.846 align:middle
but still healthy pace.

01:42.716 --> 01:44.896 align:middle
For example, last September,

01:44.896 --> 01:47.236 align:middle
Committee participants
saw growth coming in at

01:47.236 --> 01:49.696 align:middle
about 2.5 percent this year.

01:51.066 --> 01:52.886 align:middle
Data arriving since
September suggest

01:52.886 --> 01:55.336 align:middle
that growth is slowing
somewhat more than expected.

01:56.056 --> 01:58.066 align:middle
Financial conditions
tightened considerably

01:58.066 --> 01:59.046 align:middle
over the fourth quarter.

01:59.676 --> 02:01.536 align:middle
While conditions have
eased since then,

02:01.826 --> 02:05.436 align:middle
they remain less supportive of
growth than during most of 2018.

02:06.666 --> 02:09.566 align:middle
Growth has slowed in some
foreign economies-notably,

02:09.566 --> 02:10.456 align:middle
in Europe and China.

02:11.856 --> 02:15.096 align:middle
While the U.S. economy showed
little evidence of slowdown

02:15.096 --> 02:18.546 align:middle
through the end of 2018,
the limited data we have

02:18.546 --> 02:20.976 align:middle
so far this year have
been somewhat more mixed.

02:22.286 --> 02:25.876 align:middle
Unusually strong payroll job
growth in January was followed

02:25.876 --> 02:27.756 align:middle
by little growth
at all in February.

02:28.696 --> 02:30.406 align:middle
Smoothing through
these variations,

02:30.486 --> 02:32.936 align:middle
average monthly job growth
appears to have stepped

02:32.986 --> 02:37.316 align:middle
down from last year's strong
pace, but job gains remain well

02:37.316 --> 02:39.456 align:middle
above the pace necessary
to provide jobs

02:39.456 --> 02:41.216 align:middle
for new labor force entrants.

02:42.606 --> 02:44.496 align:middle
Many other labor market
indicators continue

02:44.496 --> 02:45.236 align:middle
to show strength.

02:46.486 --> 02:47.856 align:middle
Weak retail sales data

02:47.856 --> 02:50.706 align:middle
for December bounced back
considerably in January but,

02:50.706 --> 02:52.306 align:middle
on balance, seem to point

02:52.306 --> 02:54.496 align:middle
to somewhat slower growth
in consumer spending.

02:56.156 --> 02:58.446 align:middle
Business fixed investment
also appears to be growing

02:58.446 --> 03:00.086 align:middle
at a slower pace than last year.

03:01.966 --> 03:04.246 align:middle
Inflation has been
muted, and some indicators

03:04.246 --> 03:06.676 align:middle
of longer-term inflation
expectations remain

03:06.756 --> 03:09.346 align:middle
at the low end of their
ranges in recent years.

03:10.606 --> 03:13.486 align:middle
Along with these developments,
unresolved policy issues,

03:13.566 --> 03:16.216 align:middle
such as Brexit and the
ongoing trade negotiations,

03:16.216 --> 03:18.626 align:middle
pose some risks to the outlook.

03:20.256 --> 03:22.306 align:middle
Much of the discussion
at our meeting focused

03:22.306 --> 03:24.616 align:middle
on what we should make
of the varied indicators.

03:25.206 --> 03:27.606 align:middle
Today's Summary of Economic
Projections, the SEP,

03:27.906 --> 03:28.986 align:middle
reflects the assessments

03:28.986 --> 03:30.626 align:middle
of individual Committee
participants.

03:31.156 --> 03:34.396 align:middle
And these views are in line with
a broad range of other forecasts

03:34.776 --> 03:36.786 align:middle
and point to a modest slowdown,

03:36.786 --> 03:38.766 align:middle
with overall conditions
remaining favorable.

03:39.756 --> 03:43.566 align:middle
FOMC participants
sought-now see 2019 growth

03:43.566 --> 03:46.986 align:middle
at roughly 2 percent, with the
unemployment rate remaining

03:46.986 --> 03:47.956 align:middle
below 4 percent.

03:48.786 --> 03:51.666 align:middle
Core inflation, which omits
the effects of volatile food

03:51.666 --> 03:54.106 align:middle
and energy prices,
remains close to 2 percent.

03:55.246 --> 03:58.206 align:middle
Declines in oil prices
since last fall are expected

03:58.206 --> 04:00.986 align:middle
to push headline inflation
below 2 percent for a time,

04:01.446 --> 04:03.236 align:middle
but this effect is
likely to be temporary.

04:05.146 --> 04:07.566 align:middle
Now, I'm describing views
of the most likely outcomes,

04:07.566 --> 04:09.986 align:middle
but historical experience
reminds us that growth

04:09.986 --> 04:12.336 align:middle
and inflation this year
could be stronger or weaker

04:12.766 --> 04:13.816 align:middle
than what we now project.

04:14.406 --> 04:18.266 align:middle
The federal funds rate is now
in the broad range of estimates

04:18.266 --> 04:21.146 align:middle
of neutral-the rate that
tends neither to stimulate nor

04:21.146 --> 04:22.436 align:middle
to restrain the economy.

04:23.256 --> 04:25.526 align:middle
As I noted, my colleagues
and I think

04:25.596 --> 04:28.016 align:middle
that this setting is well
suited to the current outlook

04:28.646 --> 04:31.006 align:middle
and believe that we should be
patient in assessing the need

04:31.006 --> 04:32.936 align:middle
for any change in
the stance of policy.

04:34.006 --> 04:36.746 align:middle
"Patient" means that we see
no need to rush to judgment.

04:37.456 --> 04:39.826 align:middle
It may be some time
before the outlook for jobs

04:39.826 --> 04:42.626 align:middle
and inflation calls clearly
for a change in policy.

04:43.316 --> 04:46.596 align:middle
In discussing the
Committee's projections,

04:46.596 --> 04:48.906 align:middle
it's useful to note what
those projections are,

04:48.906 --> 04:50.196 align:middle
as well as what they are not.

04:51.536 --> 04:55.146 align:middle
The SEP includes participants'
individual projections

04:55.146 --> 04:58.146 align:middle
of the most likely economic
scenario, along with their views

04:58.146 --> 04:59.306 align:middle
of the appropriate path

04:59.796 --> 05:01.716 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate in that scenario.

05:03.086 --> 05:05.816 align:middle
Views about the most likely
scenario form one input

05:06.376 --> 05:07.806 align:middle
into our policy discussions.

05:08.566 --> 05:11.906 align:middle
We also discuss other plausible
scenarios, including the risk

05:11.906 --> 05:13.226 align:middle
of more worrisome outcomes.

05:13.976 --> 05:16.836 align:middle
These and other scenarios and
many other considerations go

05:16.836 --> 05:19.776 align:middle
into policy but are not
reflected in projections

05:19.776 --> 05:21.036 align:middle
of the most likely case.

05:22.086 --> 05:25.116 align:middle
Thus, we always emphasize that
the interest rate projections

05:25.116 --> 05:27.136 align:middle
in the SEP are not a
Committee decision.

05:27.356 --> 05:28.416 align:middle
They are not a Committee plan.

05:29.056 --> 05:32.546 align:middle
As Chair Yellen noted some
years ago, the FOMC statement,

05:32.766 --> 05:35.636 align:middle
rather than the dot plot, is the
device that the Committee uses

05:35.636 --> 05:38.136 align:middle
to express its opinions about
the likely path of rates.

05:38.646 --> 05:42.856 align:middle
Today the Committee released
Balance Sheet Normalization

05:42.856 --> 05:45.436 align:middle
Principles and Plans-revised
Balance Sheet Normalization

05:45.436 --> 05:46.366 align:middle
Principles and Plans.

05:46.566 --> 05:48.846 align:middle
We have long said that the size

05:48.846 --> 05:50.836 align:middle
of the balance sheet will
be considered normalized

05:51.036 --> 05:52.446 align:middle
when the balance
sheet is once again

05:52.446 --> 05:54.026 align:middle
at the smallest level consistent

05:54.026 --> 05:56.846 align:middle
with conducting monetary policy
efficiently and effectively.

05:57.356 --> 06:00.426 align:middle
We have sought to make
the normalization process

06:00.426 --> 06:03.126 align:middle
transparent, predictable,
and gradual in order

06:03.126 --> 06:06.856 align:middle
to minimize disruption and risks
to our dual-mandate objectives.

06:08.296 --> 06:10.376 align:middle
Today's announcement is
the result of discussions

06:10.376 --> 06:13.186 align:middle
over the past four FOMC
meetings about how best

06:13.186 --> 06:14.296 align:middle
to achieve these goals.

06:15.926 --> 06:18.456 align:middle
The plans have many technical
details, and I'll be happy

06:18.456 --> 06:20.216 align:middle
to answer questions
on those details.

06:20.656 --> 06:22.846 align:middle
But, for now, I'll
summarize the key elements.

06:23.806 --> 06:27.186 align:middle
Since October 2017, we have
been allowing our asset holdings

06:27.186 --> 06:30.856 align:middle
to decline by not reinvesting
all of the payments we receive

06:30.936 --> 06:35.386 align:middle
as securities matured
or were prepaid.

06:36.346 --> 06:38.606 align:middle
Today we announced that we
intend to slow the runoff

06:38.606 --> 06:42.296 align:middle
of our assets starting in May
and to cease runoff entirely

06:42.296 --> 06:43.476 align:middle
in September of this year.

06:44.676 --> 06:48.946 align:middle
In September, reserve
balances may still be somewhat

06:48.946 --> 06:50.136 align:middle
above the level required

06:50.136 --> 06:52.496 align:middle
to conduct policy
efficiently and effectively.

06:52.866 --> 06:55.276 align:middle
If this is so, we
may hold the size

06:55.276 --> 06:57.626 align:middle
of our asset holdings
roughly constant for a time.

06:58.186 --> 07:01.346 align:middle
During this time, ongoing
gradual increases in currency

07:01.736 --> 07:04.806 align:middle
and our other nonreserve
liabilities would imply very

07:04.806 --> 07:06.956 align:middle
gradual declines in
reserve balances.

07:07.606 --> 07:09.216 align:middle
When the Committee judges

07:09.216 --> 07:11.176 align:middle
that reserves should
not decline further,

07:11.636 --> 07:14.206 align:middle
securities holdings will
again begin to rise,

07:14.206 --> 07:16.776 align:middle
as dictated by the growth
of demand for our reserve

07:16.776 --> 07:18.306 align:middle
and nonreserve liabilities.

07:19.556 --> 07:22.306 align:middle
We believe that these plans
will facilitate a predictable,

07:22.306 --> 07:24.156 align:middle
transparent, and smooth process,

07:24.626 --> 07:26.416 align:middle
and we will make
additional adjustments

07:26.666 --> 07:27.706 align:middle
if conditions warrant.

07:28.326 --> 07:30.806 align:middle
The Committee will soon turn

07:30.806 --> 07:32.786 align:middle
to a few remaining
normalization topics,

07:33.206 --> 07:36.186 align:middle
including the desired maturity
composition of our portfolio

07:36.186 --> 07:37.216 align:middle
of Treasury securities.

07:38.196 --> 07:40.796 align:middle
We maintain our long-stated
intention to return

07:40.856 --> 07:44.096 align:middle
to a portfolio consisting
mainly of Treasury securities.

07:45.016 --> 07:45.986 align:middle
Thank you.

07:45.986 --> 07:48.366 align:middle
And I will be glad to
take your questions.

07:49.966 --> 07:51.316 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

07:53.676 --> 07:55.456 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

07:55.976 --> 08:00.126 align:middle
On the broader economy, can you
clarify how worried the FOMC is

08:00.126 --> 08:01.656 align:middle
about a steep slowdown?

08:01.656 --> 08:04.116 align:middle
Some of the actions today
look like there is more worry.

08:04.456 --> 08:06.426 align:middle
And on the balance
sheet, can you clarify:

08:06.426 --> 08:12.406 align:middle
Does the FOMC see the runoff as
a form of monetary tightening?

08:13.006 --> 08:14.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:15.406 --> 08:20.956 align:middle
So, on the outlook, our
outlook is a positive one.

08:21.006 --> 08:25.696 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, FOMC
participants continue

08:25.696 --> 08:27.796 align:middle
to see growth this year
of around 2 percent,

08:28.146 --> 08:33.156 align:middle
just a bit below what we saw
back in-at the end of last year.

08:34.156 --> 08:37.166 align:middle
And part of that is seeing

08:37.166 --> 08:40.616 align:middle
that economic
fundamentals-underlying economic

08:40.616 --> 08:42.956 align:middle
fundamentals are
still very strong.

08:43.176 --> 08:46.566 align:middle
You have a strong labor
market by most measures.

08:47.096 --> 08:48.366 align:middle
You have rising incomes.

08:48.526 --> 08:49.966 align:middle
You've got very low
unemployment.

08:50.736 --> 08:53.156 align:middle
You have confidence surveys
for households and also

08:53.156 --> 08:56.226 align:middle
for businesses that are
at attractive levels,

08:56.226 --> 08:57.966 align:middle
and you also have
financial conditions

08:57.966 --> 09:00.946 align:middle
that are more accommodative

09:00.946 --> 09:02.156 align:middle
than they were a
couple of months ago.

09:02.416 --> 09:05.846 align:middle
So we see the outlook
as a positive one.

09:05.926 --> 09:08.406 align:middle
As far as balance
sheet-the balance sheet plan,

09:08.536 --> 09:13.416 align:middle
the answer to that is, you
asked whether that's related

09:13.416 --> 09:15.056 align:middle
to our monetary policy,
in effect,

09:15.056 --> 09:16.346 align:middle
and the answer is really "no."

09:16.806 --> 09:21.076 align:middle
We are-we still-we think
of the interest rate tool

09:21.076 --> 09:24.946 align:middle
as the principal tool of
monetary policy, and we think

09:24.946 --> 09:28.496 align:middle
of ourselves as returning the
balance sheet to a normal level

09:28.496 --> 09:30.116 align:middle
over the course of
the next six months.

09:30.746 --> 09:33.096 align:middle
And we're not really
thinking of those

09:33.146 --> 09:34.956 align:middle
as two different tools
of monetary policy.

09:35.446 --> 09:39.046 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:40.036 --> 09:40.816 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

09:40.816 --> 09:42.516 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, can you talk

09:42.516 --> 09:46.536 align:middle
about how global developments
are affecting the U.S.?

09:46.536 --> 09:48.656 align:middle
What's the cause of the
weakness over there?

09:49.036 --> 09:53.336 align:middle
How much is it responsible for
the downgrade in GDP over here?

09:53.336 --> 09:56.646 align:middle
And what impact are tariffs,
both in the United States

09:56.646 --> 09:59.436 align:middle
and retaliatory tariffs,
having on both the U.S.

09:59.436 --> 10:00.486 align:middle
and the global economies?

10:00.486 --> 10:00.816 align:middle
Thank you.

10:01.096 --> 10:01.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:02.166 --> 10:06.436 align:middle
So the global economy
was a tailwind

10:06.436 --> 10:08.276 align:middle
for the United States in 2017.

10:08.276 --> 10:10.826 align:middle
That was the year of
synchronized global growth.

10:11.126 --> 10:15.316 align:middle
And we began 2018 expecting and
hoping for more of the same.

10:15.316 --> 10:17.656 align:middle
What happened instead is that
the global economy started

10:17.656 --> 10:20.786 align:middle
to gradually slow, and
now we see a situation

10:20.786 --> 10:25.666 align:middle
where the European economy
has slowed substantially-and

10:25.666 --> 10:26.786 align:middle
so has the Chinese economy,

10:26.786 --> 10:28.616 align:middle
although the European
economy more.

10:29.206 --> 10:32.886 align:middle
And just as strong global
growth was a tailwind,

10:33.086 --> 10:37.686 align:middle
weaker global growth can be
a headwind to our economy.

10:38.256 --> 10:39.866 align:middle
How big is that effect?

10:39.976 --> 10:42.906 align:middle
It's hard to be precise
about it, but,

10:43.186 --> 10:44.396 align:middle
clearly, we will feel that.

10:44.396 --> 10:46.296 align:middle
It is an integrated
global economy,

10:46.296 --> 10:48.306 align:middle
and global financial markets
are integrated as well.

10:49.146 --> 10:50.886 align:middle
In terms of what's causing it,

10:50.886 --> 10:54.206 align:middle
it seems to be a range
of different things.

10:54.336 --> 10:55.786 align:middle
In China, you have, you know,

10:55.836 --> 10:58.726 align:middle
factors that are very
specific to China.

10:58.896 --> 11:02.466 align:middle
The main point, though,
is that, I would say,

11:02.466 --> 11:04.306 align:middle
the outlook-let's
look at the outlook.

11:04.476 --> 11:08.246 align:middle
Chinese authorities have taken
many steps since the middle

11:08.246 --> 11:10.466 align:middle
of last year to support
economic activity,

11:11.156 --> 11:15.196 align:middle
and I think the base
case is that, ultimately,

11:15.196 --> 11:18.556 align:middle
Chinese activity will stabilize
at an attractive level.

11:19.256 --> 11:23.476 align:middle
And in Europe, you know, we
see some weakening, but, again,

11:23.476 --> 11:26.056 align:middle
we don't see-we don't
see recession,

11:26.366 --> 11:28.236 align:middle
and we do see positive
growth still.

11:28.816 --> 11:30.926 align:middle
You ask about tariffs.

11:31.446 --> 11:35.296 align:middle
I would say, tariffs may
be a factor in China.

11:35.296 --> 11:37.196 align:middle
I don't think they're
the main factor.

11:37.196 --> 11:39.896 align:middle
I think the main factors
are the delevering campaign

11:39.896 --> 11:41.826 align:middle
that the government undertook
a couple of years ago

11:41.826 --> 11:44.536 align:middle
and also just the
longer-term slowing

11:44.536 --> 11:46.446 align:middle
to a more sustainable
pace of growth

11:46.506 --> 11:49.796 align:middle
that economies find
as they mature.

11:49.796 --> 11:53.106 align:middle
In terms of our own economy,

11:53.106 --> 11:56.856 align:middle
the level of tariffs is
relatively small in the size

11:56.856 --> 12:00.066 align:middle
of our economy-relative to
the size of our economy.

12:00.726 --> 12:03.626 align:middle
We have, since the beginning
of the year-and before,

12:03.626 --> 12:06.126 align:middle
really-been hearing from
our extensive network

12:06.126 --> 12:09.206 align:middle
of business contacts a lot
of concerns about tariffs,

12:09.206 --> 12:13.266 align:middle
concerns about material costs on
imported products, and the loss

12:13.266 --> 12:15.456 align:middle
of markets and things like that,
depending on which industry,

12:15.456 --> 12:17.966 align:middle
so there's a fair
amount of uncertainty.

12:17.966 --> 12:19.326 align:middle
It's hard to say how much

12:19.326 --> 12:20.886 align:middle
of an effect that's
having on our economy.

12:21.426 --> 12:23.396 align:middle
It's very hard to
tease that apart.

12:23.396 --> 12:25.946 align:middle
But I will say, it's been a
prominent concern among our

12:25.946 --> 12:29.176 align:middle
business contacts
for some time now.

12:29.256 --> 12:31.986 align:middle
JAMES PUZZANGHERA.

12:32.626 --> 12:33.626 align:middle
Hi. Thank you, Chairman.

12:33.776 --> 12:37.646 align:middle
I want to switch gears
temporarily to Wells Fargo.

12:37.646 --> 12:39.006 align:middle
Wells Fargo last week announced

12:39.006 --> 12:42.216 align:middle
that its chief executive
received a 5 percent pay raise

12:42.216 --> 12:45.106 align:middle
last year, and this comes
among continued settlements

12:45.106 --> 12:48.276 align:middle
by the bank of various
consumer abuse and reports

12:48.336 --> 12:51.006 align:middle
that new consumer-unfriendly
sales incentives are returning.

12:51.376 --> 12:53.476 align:middle
Do you have concerns about
Wells Fargo's efforts

12:53.476 --> 12:57.066 align:middle
to fix its problems, and do you
expect the asset cap to remain

12:57.066 --> 12:58.406 align:middle
in place beyond this year?

12:58.886 --> 12:59.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:59.556 --> 13:00.386 align:middle
Wells Fargo.

13:00.696 --> 13:04.126 align:middle
So what happened at Wells
Fargo really was a remarkably

13:04.126 --> 13:07.426 align:middle
widespread series of
breakdowns, really,

13:07.496 --> 13:12.286 align:middle
in their risk-management
apparatus, which resulted

13:12.286 --> 13:15.426 align:middle
in significant consumer
abuses, let's say.

13:16.246 --> 13:18.856 align:middle
And as it's gone on and on,

13:18.856 --> 13:21.536 align:middle
it's become clear that-I think
some time ago it became clear

13:21.536 --> 13:24.226 align:middle
that these are deep problems
that needed to be addressed

13:24.896 --> 13:27.076 align:middle
in a fundamental kind of a way.

13:27.576 --> 13:29.256 align:middle
So there's a lot of
work to do on that.

13:29.456 --> 13:31.966 align:middle
And we put in place, really,
an unprecedented sanction

13:31.966 --> 13:36.916 align:middle
in the form of an asset growth
cap, and we will not lift

13:36.916 --> 13:40.286 align:middle
that until Wells Fargo gets
their arms around this,

13:40.326 --> 13:42.746 align:middle
comes forward with plans,
implements those plans,

13:42.746 --> 13:45.176 align:middle
and we're satisfied
with what they've done.

13:45.246 --> 13:47.036 align:middle
And that's not where
we are right now.

13:47.096 --> 13:47.556 align:middle
JAMES PUZZANGHERA.

13:47.746 --> 13:50.086 align:middle
Do you believe it's
appropriate-do you believe it's

13:50.086 --> 13:53.366 align:middle
appropriate for the CEO to be
getting a pay raise in light

13:53.366 --> 13:56.826 align:middle
of those conditions, in terms
of corporate governance?

13:56.826 --> 13:57.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:57.036 --> 13:59.906 align:middle
You know, our main
supervisory focus is

13:59.996 --> 14:04.476 align:middle
on the company fixing its
risk-management approach

14:04.536 --> 14:08.216 align:middle
and fundamentally
rebuilding that approach.

14:08.486 --> 14:11.156 align:middle
We don't approve
individual pay packages.

14:11.566 --> 14:15.916 align:middle
We do supervise boards of
directors for, you know,

14:16.286 --> 14:19.046 align:middle
for having a set of
compensation practices

14:19.046 --> 14:22.216 align:middle
that don't reward
short-term risk-taking

14:22.216 --> 14:23.026 align:middle
and that sort of thing.

14:23.366 --> 14:25.786 align:middle
And we will supervise
based on that.

14:27.996 --> 14:30.856 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

14:32.066 --> 14:34.066 align:middle
Michael McKee with Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

14:34.066 --> 14:36.796 align:middle
I wonder if you could
discuss the balance of risks.

14:36.796 --> 14:39.876 align:middle
I know you said that the outlook
among the Committee members is a

14:39.876 --> 14:42.096 align:middle
positive one, but since
the end of the meeting,

14:42.506 --> 14:45.866 align:middle
the 3-month-10-year
Treasury spread has fallen

14:45.866 --> 14:47.876 align:middle
to 7 basis points, and now,

14:47.876 --> 14:49.496 align:middle
according to fed
funds futures trading,

14:49.496 --> 14:53.536 align:middle
there's a 50 percent chance
of a rate cut by next January.

14:53.896 --> 14:55.336 align:middle
How far off are the markets?

14:55.336 --> 14:57.946 align:middle
What do you think the
risks actually are?

14:58.946 --> 14:59.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:59.766 --> 15:04.016 align:middle
Well, first, the data are not
currently sending a signal

15:04.016 --> 15:05.376 align:middle
that we need to move
in one direction

15:05.486 --> 15:08.236 align:middle
or another, in my view.

15:08.996 --> 15:17.216 align:middle
I would say it this way:
We see a positive outlook

15:17.216 --> 15:18.536 align:middle
for this year, a
favorable outlook

15:18.536 --> 15:19.596 align:middle
for this year, as I mentioned.

15:19.596 --> 15:24.156 align:middle
So, in our SEP projections,
Committee members, participants,

15:24.156 --> 15:26.196 align:middle
generally see growth
of around 2 percent.

15:26.586 --> 15:28.716 align:middle
They see unemployment
remaining below 4 percent.

15:29.006 --> 15:32.106 align:middle
They see inflation
remaining close to target.

15:32.166 --> 15:35.216 align:middle
And they see growth, as
I said, around 2 percent.

15:35.756 --> 15:38.336 align:middle
So, you know, that's
a positive outlook.

15:38.336 --> 15:39.266 align:middle
It's a favorable outlook.

15:39.646 --> 15:42.316 align:middle
We're also very mindful-and
we have been, of course,

15:42.896 --> 15:45.956 align:middle
all along-of what the risks are.

15:46.006 --> 15:48.076 align:middle
And you see-you mentioned
some of them.

15:48.076 --> 15:49.776 align:middle
You know, you see
slowing global growth.

15:50.296 --> 15:52.126 align:middle
You still have-there's
no resolution of Brexit.

15:52.126 --> 15:54.156 align:middle
There's no resolution,
really, of the trade talks.

15:54.446 --> 15:55.636 align:middle
These are ongoing risks.

15:55.986 --> 15:59.206 align:middle
We're also carefully
monitoring what's happening

15:59.486 --> 16:00.616 align:middle
with U.S. growth.

16:00.616 --> 16:02.256 align:middle
We called that out
in our statement.

16:02.256 --> 16:05.406 align:middle
You know, the limited data that
we have do show a slowdown.

16:05.636 --> 16:06.796 align:middle
On the other hand,
as I mentioned,

16:07.296 --> 16:09.696 align:middle
we see the underlying
economic fundamentals

16:09.696 --> 16:11.926 align:middle
for growth this year
as still very positive.

16:12.316 --> 16:15.656 align:middle
So that's really how
we're thinking about it.

16:17.286 --> 16:18.706 align:middle
JIM TANKERSLEY.

16:21.276 --> 16:22.036 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

16:22.036 --> 16:23.146 align:middle
Jim Tankersley, New York Times.

16:23.396 --> 16:27.316 align:middle
I'm curious, you're now a full
percentage point-actually,

16:27.316 --> 16:29.546 align:middle
more than a percentage
point-below the White House

16:29.606 --> 16:31.556 align:middle
in your projections
for growth this year.

16:31.616 --> 16:34.766 align:middle
By my calculations, that's
the largest spread we've seen

16:34.766 --> 16:35.836 align:middle
since the end of the recession

16:36.216 --> 16:37.996 align:middle
between the White
House and the Fed.

16:38.346 --> 16:41.556 align:middle
Why, for one, do you see-do
you think you see the economy

16:41.556 --> 16:43.716 align:middle
so differently than they
do, and do you worry at all

16:43.716 --> 16:45.396 align:middle
about implications
for policy from that?

16:46.176 --> 16:47.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:47.546 --> 16:49.046 align:middle
I haven't-I haven't
seen their projection.

16:49.046 --> 16:50.406 align:middle
I wouldn't comment
on their projection.

16:50.406 --> 16:51.716 align:middle
I would take it this way.

16:52.196 --> 16:56.216 align:middle
You can think of growth as
being composed of two things.

16:56.216 --> 16:59.616 align:middle
And one is really growth in the
workforce-more hours worked.

16:59.806 --> 17:01.746 align:middle
And the other is
productivity-it's output

17:01.746 --> 17:02.146 align:middle
per hour.

17:02.146 --> 17:05.066 align:middle
You can really think of
growth as those two things.

17:05.416 --> 17:08.026 align:middle
And I've been calling-often
mentioning these days

17:08.026 --> 17:12.526 align:middle
that it would be great if we
had national-level policies

17:12.836 --> 17:14.766 align:middle
to support higher labor
force participation.

17:14.766 --> 17:16.806 align:middle
The United States is now one

17:16.806 --> 17:19.306 align:middle
of the lowest countries
among the advanced economies,

17:19.436 --> 17:21.016 align:middle
in terms of our labor
force participation

17:21.016 --> 17:22.026 align:middle
by prime-age workers.

17:22.666 --> 17:25.376 align:middle
And that's a place where
we can grow faster.

17:25.376 --> 17:27.846 align:middle
If we can bring more
people into the labor force

17:28.476 --> 17:30.566 align:middle
and give them a chance to
contribute to and benefit

17:30.566 --> 17:31.966 align:middle
from our overall prosperity,

17:31.966 --> 17:33.266 align:middle
that will be a great
thing for the country.

17:33.266 --> 17:34.776 align:middle
So I would like to see that.

17:34.836 --> 17:36.006 align:middle
Productivity is much harder.

17:36.006 --> 17:37.656 align:middle
It's very difficult to
project productivity

17:37.656 --> 17:39.446 align:middle
over long stretches of time.

17:39.446 --> 17:41.116 align:middle
It's a function of
evolving technology.

17:41.506 --> 17:44.356 align:middle
So, I guess I would say, what
is the potential growth rate?

17:46.106 --> 17:50.206 align:middle
It's quite hard to know with
any precision, and I just would

17:50.206 --> 17:53.026 align:middle
like to see us, you
know, undertake an effort

17:53.026 --> 17:56.556 align:middle
to make it be as high as
it can sustainably be.

17:56.556 --> 17:56.906 align:middle
JIM TANKERSLEY.

17:56.906 --> 17:58.446 align:middle
A quick follow-up on that.

17:58.446 --> 18:02.896 align:middle
Do you see the tax
cuts-the 2017 tax cuts

18:02.976 --> 18:06.456 align:middle
as having provided a large boost
to labor force participation

18:06.546 --> 18:07.366 align:middle
as the White House does?

18:07.366 --> 18:08.386 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:08.596 --> 18:09.346 align:middle
I would say so.

18:09.446 --> 18:14.256 align:middle
I think it's clear that the
tax and spending policies

18:14.256 --> 18:17.706 align:middle
that were adopted early
last year supported demand

18:17.706 --> 18:19.826 align:middle
in a significant way last year.

18:20.476 --> 18:24.096 align:middle
And it's also the case, I think,

18:24.096 --> 18:25.836 align:middle
that they should have
some supply-side effects.

18:25.836 --> 18:28.676 align:middle
I think it's hard to know,
it's hard to identify those

18:28.676 --> 18:32.636 align:middle
with any precision, and we
hope they're-we hope they're

18:32.636 --> 18:33.736 align:middle
very large.

18:33.836 --> 18:34.776 align:middle
You know, the idea would be

18:34.996 --> 18:37.446 align:middle
that lowering corporate taxes
would spur more corporate

18:37.446 --> 18:39.226 align:middle
investment, which would
spur more productivity,

18:39.746 --> 18:41.836 align:middle
and lowering individual taxes
would spur greater labor

18:41.836 --> 18:42.656 align:middle
force participation.

18:43.296 --> 18:45.116 align:middle
I wouldn't want to
be handing-you know,

18:45.276 --> 18:47.826 align:middle
assigning credit or blame
for that, but I do think

18:47.826 --> 18:50.466 align:middle
that the performance of
labor force participation

18:51.026 --> 18:52.956 align:middle
over the last, really, three

18:52.956 --> 18:54.966 align:middle
or four years has been
an upside surprise

18:55.046 --> 18:56.476 align:middle
that most people
didn't see coming,

18:56.476 --> 18:57.586 align:middle
and it's extremely welcome.

18:58.386 --> 18:59.976 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

19:00.656 --> 19:04.776 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

19:05.156 --> 19:06.836 align:middle
With the new dot plot today,

19:06.836 --> 19:11.286 align:middle
you've gone from two rate
hikes in 2019 to zero.

19:11.626 --> 19:15.606 align:middle
You still have one
showing for next year.

19:16.396 --> 19:21.056 align:middle
The fed funds futures trading,
as has been alluded to,

19:21.056 --> 19:25.666 align:middle
is showing rate cuts at
the end of this year.

19:25.666 --> 19:28.346 align:middle
Are they-is that a
possibility in your mind,

19:28.346 --> 19:31.906 align:middle
given the sharp change
you've made at this meeting?

19:32.736 --> 19:33.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:33.286 --> 19:34.696 align:middle
As I mentioned, the data

19:34.696 --> 19:37.256 align:middle
that we're seeing are not
currently sending a signal

19:37.346 --> 19:40.166 align:middle
which suggests moving in
either direction for me,

19:40.166 --> 19:42.136 align:middle
which is really why
we're being patient.

19:42.706 --> 19:45.786 align:middle
We feel our policy rate is
in the range of neutral.

19:45.786 --> 19:47.686 align:middle
The economy is growing
at about trend.

19:47.876 --> 19:49.216 align:middle
Inflation is close to target.

19:49.626 --> 19:51.006 align:middle
Unemployment is under 3 percent.

19:51.496 --> 19:54.326 align:middle
It's a great time for us to
be patient and watch and wait

19:54.326 --> 19:55.336 align:middle
and see how things evolve.

19:57.446 --> 19:59.656 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

19:59.786 --> 20:01.346 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

20:01.736 --> 20:06.026 align:middle
On stress testing, I wanted
to ask, how do you respond

20:06.026 --> 20:07.966 align:middle
to criticism that
the Fed's recent move

20:07.966 --> 20:09.586 align:middle
to remove the qualitative
objection

20:09.586 --> 20:11.956 align:middle
from CCAR makes it
less transparent

20:12.046 --> 20:13.076 align:middle
to the general public?

20:13.446 --> 20:16.916 align:middle
And then, also, Governor
Brainard dissented

20:16.916 --> 20:18.706 align:middle
on that decision,
and she's dissented

20:18.706 --> 20:22.156 align:middle
on some other decisions as
well regulatorily recently.

20:22.656 --> 20:24.306 align:middle
Does it concern you, given

20:24.306 --> 20:26.596 align:middle
that the Fed has traditionally
been a consensus-based

20:26.596 --> 20:30.596 align:middle
organization, if you keep having
these decisions where, you know,

20:30.596 --> 20:33.866 align:middle
a Board member is not
onboard with them?

20:34.366 --> 20:35.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:35.276 --> 20:38.156 align:middle
So you mentioned the
qualitative aspect of CCAR.

20:38.756 --> 20:43.466 align:middle
And U.S.-the large U.S.
financial institutions have made

20:43.736 --> 20:47.156 align:middle
significant progress there
and are now much better

20:47.156 --> 20:48.636 align:middle
at the capital planning process.

20:49.356 --> 20:53.106 align:middle
And for, really, some time
now, for a couple of years,

20:53.106 --> 20:55.176 align:middle
we've talked about
moving supervision

20:55.176 --> 20:58.536 align:middle
of that process-moving
to a supervisory approach

20:58.536 --> 21:01.146 align:middle
to that rather than having a
pass/fail in the stress tests.

21:01.146 --> 21:02.646 align:middle
We've been talking about
that for quite a while.

21:03.136 --> 21:06.846 align:middle
And I think, given the level
that the banks have moved

21:06.846 --> 21:09.116 align:middle
to in capital planning,
we did that.

21:09.206 --> 21:10.836 align:middle
It shouldn't be a big
surprise-something we've been

21:10.836 --> 21:12.426 align:middle
talking about doing
for a couple of years.

21:12.956 --> 21:15.516 align:middle
For banks that are newer to
CCAR and haven't made that kind

21:15.516 --> 21:16.816 align:middle
of progress, then
they're still going

21:16.816 --> 21:20.456 align:middle
to be part of-they're still
going to have a qualitative test

21:20.456 --> 21:23.226 align:middle
until they can reach similar
levels of achievement.

21:23.806 --> 21:27.036 align:middle
In terms of having
disparate views, I-look,

21:27.036 --> 21:30.886 align:middle
we try-we are very much a
consensus-driven organization.

21:30.886 --> 21:33.186 align:middle
We try very hard to reach
consensus on things.

21:33.546 --> 21:38.496 align:middle
In the end, it's very healthy
to have disparate views

21:38.926 --> 21:41.156 align:middle
and to state them publicly
and put them on the record.

21:41.156 --> 21:42.896 align:middle
It's nothing but healthy
from my standpoint.

21:43.396 --> 21:45.656 align:middle
We'll always be trying to
reach consensus, and, you know,

21:45.656 --> 21:47.856 align:middle
I'm committed to that.

21:48.986 --> 21:50.616 align:middle
TREVOR HUNNICUTT.

21:51.416 --> 21:53.126 align:middle
Trevor Hunnicutt from Reuters.

21:53.446 --> 21:55.486 align:middle
Just-you mentioned
that you have, kind of,

21:55.486 --> 21:57.796 align:middle
a positive outlook as
it regards the economy

21:57.796 --> 22:01.166 align:middle
but also see slower growth
on the household side

22:01.166 --> 22:02.856 align:middle
and the business side.

22:02.906 --> 22:05.846 align:middle
Given how big of a part of
the U.S. economy that is,

22:06.236 --> 22:07.616 align:middle
what gives you, kind of,

22:07.616 --> 22:09.846 align:middle
confidence that both the
slowdown we're seeing

22:10.006 --> 22:10.696 align:middle
is temporary?

22:11.206 --> 22:12.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:12.136 --> 22:15.816 align:middle
So on the household side, what
we saw was a very weak reading

22:15.816 --> 22:18.766 align:middle
on retail sales in December
and then a bounceback

22:18.766 --> 22:19.786 align:middle
in the January reading.

22:20.046 --> 22:22.616 align:middle
And, you know, it was a
surprise, I would say,

22:23.086 --> 22:24.476 align:middle
and inconsistent

22:24.476 --> 22:26.266 align:middle
with a significant
amount of other data.

22:26.266 --> 22:29.886 align:middle
We're not dismissing it in any
way, but I would go back to,

22:31.116 --> 22:33.656 align:middle
what is it that supports
consumer spending?

22:33.656 --> 22:37.296 align:middle
It's 70 percent of the
economy, as you point out.

22:37.296 --> 22:41.916 align:middle
It's strong economic underlying
fundamentals, so, rising wages,

22:41.966 --> 22:45.526 align:middle
high levels of employment,
low levels of unemployment,

22:45.526 --> 22:49.136 align:middle
high levels of job
creation, confidence.

22:49.136 --> 22:51.786 align:middle
The household confidence
surveys have moved back up to

22:51.786 --> 22:52.876 align:middle
where they were last summer.

22:53.196 --> 22:55.026 align:middle
So we look at those
fundamentals, and we think

22:55.756 --> 22:57.806 align:middle
that looks like a setting

22:57.806 --> 23:01.496 align:middle
in which consumption
will have support

23:01.546 --> 23:04.616 align:middle
from underlying economic
fundamentals.

23:04.616 --> 23:06.056 align:middle
And that's really what
we're thinking there.

23:07.156 --> 23:10.356 align:middle
So, you know, we're
also patiently watching

23:10.356 --> 23:11.796 align:middle
and waiting and not assuming.

23:11.796 --> 23:13.936 align:middle
We're not taking no signal
from the incoming data;

23:13.936 --> 23:15.496 align:middle
that's why we called it
out in our statement.

23:16.206 --> 23:18.106 align:middle
So I think our eyes
are open on this.

23:19.706 --> 23:20.926 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

23:22.096 --> 23:24.366 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

23:25.296 --> 23:28.736 align:middle
Chair Powell, in 1998 the
Fed eased policy in a way

23:28.976 --> 23:30.726 align:middle
that some say may have
avoided a recession,

23:30.726 --> 23:34.216 align:middle
others say may have helped fuel
the NASDAQ tech stock bubble.

23:34.786 --> 23:37.696 align:middle
And financial conditions have
eased considerably this year

23:38.236 --> 23:41.746 align:middle
since the policy pivot that
you made clear in January.

23:41.746 --> 23:45.076 align:middle
The S&amp;P, for example, is
just 3 percent below last

23:45.076 --> 23:45.806 align:middle
summer's peak.

23:46.226 --> 23:49.696 align:middle
And so, I wonder, does this
episode from 20 years ago bear

23:49.696 --> 23:53.756 align:middle
at all on your thinking
today about the risks posed

23:53.796 --> 23:56.736 align:middle
by rising asset values
in an environment

23:57.096 --> 23:58.936 align:middle
of a shallower policy path?

23:59.646 --> 24:01.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:01.276 --> 24:04.716 align:middle
We're in a very different
world today and post-crisis,

24:04.946 --> 24:09.896 align:middle
because we now very carefully
monitor financial conditions

24:09.896 --> 24:13.176 align:middle
and financial stability
concerns on an ongoing basis,

24:13.176 --> 24:15.056 align:middle
and we publish a
report twice a year,

24:15.446 --> 24:18.286 align:middle
and we have quarterly Board
meetings and briefings

24:18.286 --> 24:20.056 align:middle
where we look deeply
into these things.

24:20.056 --> 24:22.666 align:middle
So this is something we have
very much on our radar screen.

24:24.156 --> 24:27.426 align:middle
And I would say, overall,

24:27.426 --> 24:30.176 align:middle
we don't see financial stability
vulnerabilities as high.

24:30.176 --> 24:33.566 align:middle
There are some aspects
of the financial markets

24:33.566 --> 24:38.146 align:middle
that we're carefully monitoring,
and those are in the nature

24:38.486 --> 24:40.776 align:middle
of things that might
be amplifiers

24:40.776 --> 24:43.286 align:middle
to a downturn-as opposed to a
financial stability concern,

24:43.286 --> 24:45.756 align:middle
which might lead to a
financial crisis and that kind

24:45.756 --> 24:46.816 align:middle
of thing, which we don't see.

24:47.436 --> 24:49.436 align:middle
So we do monitor that.

24:50.156 --> 24:53.706 align:middle
And I would also say, you
know, that the whole question

24:53.706 --> 24:56.666 align:middle
of monetary policy and financial
stability is an unsettled

24:56.666 --> 24:58.636 align:middle
and difficult one in our world.

24:59.206 --> 25:02.476 align:middle
We do think that the
principal tools for, you know,

25:02.476 --> 25:06.416 align:middle
for managing financial stability
are regulation, supervision,

25:06.416 --> 25:09.106 align:middle
macroprudential tools, and
those sorts of things as opposed

25:09.106 --> 25:10.196 align:middle
to changing the interest rate.

25:10.326 --> 25:12.866 align:middle
But we're certainly very mindful

25:12.866 --> 25:14.906 align:middle
of financial conditions
and those risks.

25:15.166 --> 25:15.846 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

25:15.846 --> 25:20.456 align:middle
If I could ask a follow-up-if
it's the case that we're

25:20.456 --> 25:22.496 align:middle
in a lower neutral
interest rate world

25:22.826 --> 25:27.376 align:middle
where you could have more
asset price appreciation,

25:27.746 --> 25:30.656 align:middle
do you think the Fed needs
more macroprudential tools

25:30.656 --> 25:31.756 align:middle
so that it doesn't have to lean

25:31.756 --> 25:33.486 align:middle
on monetary policy
to do so much?

25:34.586 --> 25:35.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:35.316 --> 25:37.856 align:middle
It's a very difficult
question with a long answer.

25:39.116 --> 25:44.916 align:middle
We-in our system, we mainly rely
on "through the cycle" tools

25:44.916 --> 25:47.446 align:middle
like high capital
and stress tests.

25:47.446 --> 25:51.856 align:middle
Our financial stability system
is built on those tools:

25:52.076 --> 25:53.546 align:middle
high capital, high liquidity,

25:54.006 --> 25:56.036 align:middle
resolution planning,
stress testing.

25:56.036 --> 25:57.186 align:middle
So those are always on.

25:57.916 --> 25:59.416 align:middle
We also have some tools,

25:59.416 --> 26:02.636 align:middle
like the countercyclical capital
buffer, which we can deploy

26:02.636 --> 26:05.196 align:middle
at times when vulnerabilities
are meaningfully above normal.

26:05.226 --> 26:08.666 align:middle
But we do rely on those tools.

26:08.666 --> 26:11.626 align:middle
And I would say, our banks
are well capitalized.

26:12.166 --> 26:15.666 align:middle
They're far better capitalized
and better aware of their risks

26:15.966 --> 26:18.866 align:middle
and more liquid than they were
before the financial crisis.

26:18.866 --> 26:21.156 align:middle
So they'll be more
resilient in, you know,

26:21.216 --> 26:22.956 align:middle
in difficult states
of the economy.

26:22.956 --> 26:24.836 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

26:25.006 --> 26:27.876 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

26:28.006 --> 26:33.286 align:middle
Included in the President's
fiscal 2020 budget is a new

26:33.286 --> 26:35.676 align:middle
estimate that the nation's
debt will balloon to more

26:35.676 --> 26:39.596 align:middle
than $31 trillion over
the next decade, by 2029.

26:40.106 --> 26:42.396 align:middle
You've previously said
that the country is

26:42.396 --> 26:44.406 align:middle
on an unsustainable fiscal path.

26:44.856 --> 26:46.646 align:middle
How alarming is that
number to you?

26:47.066 --> 26:49.986 align:middle
And, while the United States
might not be quite there yet,

26:49.986 --> 26:52.496 align:middle
when do you begin to worry
about a possible credit crisis?

26:52.796 --> 26:53.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:54.066 --> 26:57.006 align:middle
I do think that deficits matter,

26:57.006 --> 26:59.896 align:middle
and I do think-I think it's
not really controversial to say

26:59.896 --> 27:04.126 align:middle
that our debt can't grow faster
than our economy indefinitely

27:04.126 --> 27:05.066 align:middle
and that's what it's doing now.

27:05.066 --> 27:06.996 align:middle
So this is something we
will have to deal with,

27:06.996 --> 27:10.066 align:middle
and I think we can't
really lose sight of that,

27:10.156 --> 27:13.056 align:middle
and I'd like to see a greater
focus on that over time.

27:13.676 --> 27:16.976 align:middle
It's not in the nature of
a near-term debt crisis

27:16.976 --> 27:19.106 align:middle
or anything like that, and I
wouldn't want to try to predict

27:19.106 --> 27:23.776 align:middle
when that would happen, but it
is something that it's important

27:23.776 --> 27:29.916 align:middle
that the public discussion
really come back to,

27:30.046 --> 27:30.866 align:middle
if I can say that.

27:31.486 --> 27:34.006 align:middle
And we will have to
deal with it eventually.

27:36.236 --> 27:37.656 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

27:37.656 --> 27:39.386 align:middle
James Politi of the
Financial Times.

27:39.436 --> 27:41.886 align:middle
There's huge uncertainty
at the moment over the fate

27:41.886 --> 27:44.336 align:middle
of the Brexit negotiations.

27:44.336 --> 27:47.176 align:middle
How much of a factor has
this been for the Fed

27:47.176 --> 27:49.396 align:middle
in turning towards a
kind of patient approach

27:49.396 --> 27:52.256 align:middle
to monetary policy, and
what's your base case on that?

27:52.496 --> 27:54.656 align:middle
And on the size of
the balance sheet,

27:54.976 --> 27:57.896 align:middle
what's your-do you have
a numerical estimate

27:57.996 --> 27:59.756 align:middle
for where it will be at the end

27:59.756 --> 28:01.826 align:middle
of September once the
runoff is complete?

28:01.826 --> 28:02.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:02.376 --> 28:06.716 align:middle
So, of course, we're watching
Brexit carefully and hoping

28:06.716 --> 28:09.756 align:middle
that it can be resolved
in an orderly way.

28:10.456 --> 28:14.926 align:middle
From our standpoint, the part
of it that we can control is

28:15.826 --> 28:18.056 align:middle
that we've been involved

28:18.056 --> 28:20.856 align:middle
with supervising our
financial institutions

28:20.886 --> 28:23.356 align:middle
that are active either
in the United Kingdom

28:23.356 --> 28:26.446 align:middle
or the European Union or both
to make sure that they're ready

28:26.516 --> 28:30.826 align:middle
for the full range of possible
outcomes to the-to Brexit.

28:30.986 --> 28:36.546 align:middle
So-and in doing so, we've also
had-we've also worked alongside

28:36.546 --> 28:38.476 align:middle
regulators from the
United Kingdom and the EU.

28:38.546 --> 28:43.736 align:middle
So we do, again, hope that
that can be resolved well,

28:43.736 --> 28:47.886 align:middle
but we know that our banks are
well capitalized and resilient

28:47.886 --> 28:48.936 align:middle
to different kinds of events.

28:49.866 --> 28:51.546 align:middle
In terms of the size
of the balance sheet,

28:52.936 --> 28:58.256 align:middle
the balance sheet will be of a
size of approximately 17 percent

28:58.256 --> 29:02.406 align:middle
of GDP around the end of this
year, down from 25 percent

29:02.406 --> 29:06.026 align:middle
of GDP at the end of 2014-so,

29:06.026 --> 29:09.266 align:middle
significantly smaller
relative to GDP than it was.

29:10.026 --> 29:12.266 align:middle
I'm guessing you're looking
for a dollar number, though,

29:12.266 --> 29:14.476 align:middle
probably, and that would
be-so, for the size

29:14.476 --> 29:17.606 align:middle
of the balance sheet, it
looks like it'll be a bit

29:17.606 --> 29:21.426 align:middle
above $3.5 trillion then.

29:21.656 --> 29:24.606 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

29:24.946 --> 29:26.296 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

29:26.876 --> 29:29.296 align:middle
Chairman Powell, could you
talk through your outlook

29:29.296 --> 29:30.746 align:middle
for inflation this year?

29:31.486 --> 29:33.746 align:middle
Aren't-you're not
concerned about it?

29:33.746 --> 29:38.526 align:middle
And rising-why aren't rising
wages feeding into inflation?

29:39.566 --> 29:40.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:40.686 --> 29:43.556 align:middle
Let me take wages
first, if I can.

29:43.556 --> 29:48.526 align:middle
So wages have moved up in
the last couple of years

29:48.526 --> 29:51.436 align:middle
and are now running at
healthier, higher levels,

29:51.526 --> 29:53.726 align:middle
and that's a good thing.

29:53.726 --> 29:57.066 align:middle
In fact, a lot of the
wage gains have been going

29:57.066 --> 30:00.486 align:middle
to lower-paid workers, as
can happen late in the cycle,

30:00.536 --> 30:01.586 align:middle
which is also a good thing.

30:02.176 --> 30:04.686 align:middle
So-but that's not
price inflation,

30:04.686 --> 30:05.566 align:middle
that's wage inflation.

30:05.566 --> 30:08.536 align:middle
Our mandate, sorry,
is price inflation.

30:09.146 --> 30:13.616 align:middle
So what I see is inflation
that's close to 2 percent

30:14.246 --> 30:17.186 align:middle
but that sort of keeps
bumping up against 2 percent

30:17.186 --> 30:20.096 align:middle
and then maybe moving
back down a little bit.

30:20.096 --> 30:22.616 align:middle
And I don't feel
that we have kind

30:22.706 --> 30:26.826 align:middle
of convincingly achieved
our 2 percent mandate

30:26.826 --> 30:27.746 align:middle
in a symmetrical way.

30:27.876 --> 30:29.066 align:middle
Now, what do we mean
by "symmetrical"?

30:29.066 --> 30:33.376 align:middle
What we really mean is that
we would look at-we know

30:33.376 --> 30:37.496 align:middle
that inflation will move around
on both sides of the target,

30:37.496 --> 30:40.286 align:middle
and what we say is that we
would be equally concerned

30:40.286 --> 30:41.566 align:middle
with inflation persistently

30:41.566 --> 30:43.426 align:middle
above as persistently
below the target.

30:43.586 --> 30:45.056 align:middle
So that's really our framework.

30:45.726 --> 30:48.156 align:middle
And I don't think we've
quite achieved that yet,

30:48.156 --> 30:50.296 align:middle
because we're really
10 years deep

30:50.296 --> 30:53.866 align:middle
in this-almost 10
years-in this expansion,

30:53.866 --> 30:59.146 align:middle
and inflation is still kind
of, I'd say, not, you know,

30:59.456 --> 31:01.736 align:middle
clearly meeting our target.

31:01.736 --> 31:04.026 align:middle
So that's one of the reasons
why we're being patient.

31:04.026 --> 31:07.026 align:middle
I think, as I've said
before, I think inflation

31:07.026 --> 31:10.406 align:middle
that is a little bit below our
target-particularly headline

31:10.406 --> 31:12.586 align:middle
inflation this year
will be, you know,

31:12.676 --> 31:14.556 align:middle
meaningfully below our
target for most of the year

31:14.556 --> 31:17.146 align:middle
because of lower oil
prices, but we project

31:17.146 --> 31:20.866 align:middle
that core will be too-that gives
us the ability to be patient

31:20.866 --> 31:24.376 align:middle
and not move until we see

31:24.376 --> 31:26.556 align:middle
that our target goals
are being achieved.

31:27.636 --> 31:28.026 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

31:28.026 --> 31:32.206 align:middle
I just wanted to press
a little bit about,

31:32.206 --> 31:35.126 align:middle
what is the story the
Committee-you know, when you get

31:35.476 --> 31:38.866 align:middle
in the discussion today and
yesterday about inflation,

31:38.966 --> 31:42.006 align:middle
what kind of is the
story that emerges?

31:42.006 --> 31:42.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:42.466 --> 31:44.156 align:middle
So there are a bunch
of different stories.

31:44.156 --> 31:45.336 align:middle
There's no real easy answer.

31:45.336 --> 31:47.066 align:middle
One of them is just
that the natural rate

31:47.066 --> 31:48.726 align:middle
of unemployment is
lower than people think.

31:48.856 --> 31:50.116 align:middle
That's one way to
think about it,

31:50.116 --> 31:51.806 align:middle
that there's still more
slack in the economy.

31:52.416 --> 31:55.236 align:middle
Another is that expectations
play a very-inflation

31:55.236 --> 31:58.726 align:middle
expectations play a very
key role in our framework

31:58.726 --> 32:02.206 align:middle
and other frameworks, and, you
know, there is the possibility

32:02.206 --> 32:03.166 align:middle
that some people discuss

32:03.166 --> 32:06.966 align:middle
of expectations being
anchored but below 2 percent.

32:07.286 --> 32:12.326 align:middle
And so, either way, inflation
itself has kind of bounced

32:12.326 --> 32:13.926 align:middle
around a little below 2 percent.

32:14.386 --> 32:15.756 align:middle
That's the record.

32:15.756 --> 32:16.366 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

32:17.726 --> 32:18.996 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

32:20.566 --> 32:22.636 align:middle
Thanks for the question.

32:22.846 --> 32:24.866 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

32:25.316 --> 32:27.316 align:middle
I'm just kind of curious-I mean,

32:27.316 --> 32:29.956 align:middle
this below-target inflation
is a global phenomenon,

32:29.956 --> 32:34.796 align:middle
at least across advanced
economies, and I'd just

32:34.796 --> 32:36.256 align:middle
like to kind of hear your
thoughts about what kind

32:36.256 --> 32:41.296 align:middle
of challenges that poses to
policymakers like yourself

32:41.966 --> 32:45.166 align:middle
and the global economy
in general.

32:45.216 --> 32:45.556 align:middle
Yes, thanks.

32:45.596 --> 32:46.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:47.606 --> 32:49.896 align:middle
It's a major challenge.

32:49.946 --> 32:52.636 align:middle
It's one of the major
challenges of our time, really,

32:52.636 --> 32:56.826 align:middle
to have inflation-you
know, downward pressure

32:56.826 --> 32:57.696 align:middle
on inflation, let's say.

32:57.756 --> 33:01.326 align:middle
It gives central banks
less room to, you know,

33:01.916 --> 33:03.846 align:middle
to respond to downturns, right?

33:03.846 --> 33:07.096 align:middle
So if inflation expectations
are below 2 percent,

33:07.156 --> 33:09.096 align:middle
they're always going to
be pulling inflation down,

33:09.456 --> 33:12.666 align:middle
and we're going to be paddling
upstream in trying to, you know,

33:12.666 --> 33:15.666 align:middle
keep inflation at 2 percent,
which gives us some room to cut,

33:16.096 --> 33:19.636 align:middle
you know, when it's time to cut
rates when the economy weakens.

33:20.206 --> 33:24.856 align:middle
And, you know, that's something
that central banks face all

33:24.856 --> 33:27.436 align:middle
over the world, and we
certainly face that problem too.

33:28.236 --> 33:31.256 align:middle
It's one of the-one of
the things we're looking

33:31.256 --> 33:34.036 align:middle
into as part of our strategic
monetary policy review

33:34.036 --> 33:34.446 align:middle
this year.

33:35.096 --> 33:37.606 align:middle
The proximity to the
zero lower bound calls

33:37.656 --> 33:41.636 align:middle
for more creative thinking
about ways we can, you know,

33:41.716 --> 33:45.626 align:middle
uphold the credibility of our
inflation target, and, you know,

33:46.316 --> 33:51.196 align:middle
we're open minded about
ways we can do that.

33:51.796 --> 33:54.586 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

33:54.586 --> 33:56.616 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business Network.

33:56.616 --> 33:59.896 align:middle
So I counted five downgrades
in the first paragraph

33:59.896 --> 34:01.976 align:middle
of the FOMC statement.

34:02.256 --> 34:05.296 align:middle
What conditions do you need
to see in order for a rate cut

34:05.296 --> 34:07.236 align:middle
or a rate hike on the
other side of that then?

34:07.946 --> 34:08.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:08.346 --> 34:11.406 align:middle
Well, we-I think we were-we
wanted to be careful to go ahead

34:11.456 --> 34:13.276 align:middle
and acknowledge the things.

34:13.276 --> 34:15.906 align:middle
I think it's relatively little
hard data so far this year,

34:15.906 --> 34:17.566 align:middle
but we were careful to point

34:17.566 --> 34:20.826 align:middle
out lower retail sales-the
weak November meeting-BFI,

34:20.896 --> 34:23.016 align:middle
all those things, and I think
that was the right thing to do.

34:23.306 --> 34:26.446 align:middle
Notwithstanding that, we do have
a positive outlook for the year

34:26.446 --> 34:29.036 align:middle
for the reasons I
mentioned-you know,

34:29.186 --> 34:31.716 align:middle
solid underlying economic
fundamentals, et cetera.

34:32.146 --> 34:36.126 align:middle
So, and in terms of what it
would take, I'd say, again,

34:36.126 --> 34:39.776 align:middle
today we don't see any-we don't
see data coming in that suggests

34:39.776 --> 34:41.486 align:middle
that we should move
in either direction.

34:41.866 --> 34:44.136 align:middle
They suggest that we
should remain patient

34:44.136 --> 34:46.466 align:middle
and let the situation
clarify itself over time.

34:47.306 --> 34:49.486 align:middle
When the time comes,
we'll act appropriately.

34:51.446 --> 34:52.396 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

34:53.386 --> 34:55.236 align:middle
Hi. Matthew Boesler
at Bloomberg.

34:55.726 --> 34:58.876 align:middle
Fed Vice Chairman Rich Clarida
has talked about how the decline

34:58.876 --> 35:00.206 align:middle
in labor's share of income

35:00.206 --> 35:04.116 align:middle
and the corresponding high
profit margins might mean

35:04.116 --> 35:05.056 align:middle
that lower unemployment

35:05.056 --> 35:06.926 align:middle
and higher wage growth
is not flowing

35:06.926 --> 35:09.206 align:middle
through to price inflation
the way it used to.

35:10.326 --> 35:11.876 align:middle
But, so far throughout
this tightening cycle,

35:11.876 --> 35:14.566 align:middle
the Fed has not allowed
wage growth to rise

35:14.566 --> 35:17.146 align:middle
above longer-term interest
rates until just very recently.

35:17.146 --> 35:20.356 align:middle
So I'm wondering if, going
forward, given these insights

35:20.356 --> 35:23.776 align:middle
about the labor share and high
profit margins and the linkage

35:23.816 --> 35:26.806 align:middle
between wages and prices,
would you be in favor

35:26.806 --> 35:29.296 align:middle
of allowing wage growth
to continue accelerating

35:29.656 --> 35:31.866 align:middle
without matching that with
higher interest rates?

35:31.956 --> 35:33.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:33.046 --> 35:36.486 align:middle
Well, let me say that we've had,
you know, a significant move

35:36.486 --> 35:39.586 align:middle
up in wages and compensation
over the last few years,

35:39.656 --> 35:43.396 align:middle
and I-which does not trouble me

35:43.396 --> 35:44.916 align:middle
from the standpoint
of inflation.

35:45.686 --> 35:47.666 align:middle
We've had-also had-in
other cycles,

35:47.666 --> 35:50.746 align:middle
we've had situations
where, you know,

35:51.126 --> 35:53.906 align:middle
unit labor costs were
moving up above inflation,

35:54.126 --> 35:56.846 align:middle
and that didn't lead
to price inflation.

35:56.846 --> 36:00.216 align:middle
It does-you know, in theory, it
can squeeze corporate margins,

36:00.216 --> 36:02.426 align:middle
and, you know, that
can't go on indefinitely.

36:02.496 --> 36:06.656 align:middle
But, nonetheless, I don't
see the current wage picture

36:07.026 --> 36:09.506 align:middle
as concerning from an
inflation standpoint.

36:10.406 --> 36:13.126 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

36:13.126 --> 36:16.016 align:middle
More generally, to the extent
that wage growth in excess

36:16.016 --> 36:18.076 align:middle
of interest rates
allows households to pay

36:18.076 --> 36:21.086 align:middle
down debt faster, whereas
interest rates in excess

36:21.086 --> 36:24.706 align:middle
of wage growth risks a
further buildup in debt,

36:25.046 --> 36:26.706 align:middle
how do you account for the sort

36:26.706 --> 36:28.206 align:middle
of financial stability
consequences

36:28.206 --> 36:30.726 align:middle
of varying interest rates
relative to wage growth?

36:31.396 --> 36:31.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:31.606 --> 36:34.296 align:middle
I don't think we look at-I
understand what you're asking,

36:34.296 --> 36:38.236 align:middle
but we're looking at-our
mandate is price inflation

36:38.236 --> 36:40.366 align:middle
and maximum employment, and
that's what we're looking

36:40.366 --> 36:41.956 align:middle
at with setting interest rates.

36:42.396 --> 36:44.766 align:middle
And we also monitor financial
stability very carefully

36:44.766 --> 36:47.136 align:middle
across all meaningful
asset categories.

36:47.296 --> 36:49.806 align:middle
I don't think we
tie it particularly

36:49.806 --> 36:51.946 align:middle
to the relationship
you're talking about.

36:53.026 --> 36:54.616 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

36:55.896 --> 36:58.086 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

36:58.456 --> 37:01.006 align:middle
Just a quick follow-up
on Brexit.

37:01.006 --> 37:02.766 align:middle
You mentioned that
you're making sure

37:02.766 --> 37:06.806 align:middle
that U.S. financial institutions
are ready for whatever outcome.

37:06.806 --> 37:09.396 align:middle
I'm wondering, can you be a
little more specific about that?

37:09.806 --> 37:12.226 align:middle
And also, how are you
preparing for any pressures

37:12.226 --> 37:14.616 align:middle
that a hard Brexit would
put on the U.S. dollar?

37:14.956 --> 37:15.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:16.086 --> 37:20.246 align:middle
Well, as I mentioned, you
know, with the stress tests

37:20.246 --> 37:23.326 align:middle
that the largest financial
institutions-and those are the

37:23.326 --> 37:25.616 align:middle
ones that tend to be active
internationally-that they

37:25.616 --> 37:28.316 align:middle
undergo every year, we put them

37:28.316 --> 37:32.076 align:middle
through very large financial
shocks with large losses

37:32.076 --> 37:34.186 align:middle
and big changes in
markets every year.

37:34.186 --> 37:35.946 align:middle
And we vary that every year.

37:35.946 --> 37:38.976 align:middle
So that's a pretty
good-you know,

37:39.166 --> 37:41.516 align:middle
having done that for
a number of years now,

37:41.516 --> 37:44.636 align:middle
that's-and having
them be required

37:44.636 --> 37:46.886 align:middle
to have adequate capital
and liquidity even

37:46.886 --> 37:48.116 align:middle
after all that happens.

37:48.116 --> 37:50.786 align:middle
So that's a good thing to have
done, knowing that you're going

37:50.786 --> 37:52.126 align:middle
into something that's
quite unknown,

37:52.866 --> 37:55.826 align:middle
which may prove stressful,
it may not prove stressful,

37:55.826 --> 37:57.026 align:middle
depending on what
the outcome is.

37:57.026 --> 38:01.156 align:middle
So I think all that has probably
prepared our institutions well.

38:01.386 --> 38:06.586 align:middle
That said, nothing like this
has happened in recent years,

38:06.586 --> 38:08.116 align:middle
and so it's really
hard to be confident.

38:08.116 --> 38:11.006 align:middle
So we're very watchful
about what's going on.

38:11.006 --> 38:13.326 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

38:13.326 --> 38:14.416 align:middle
And as far as the dollar goes?

38:14.666 --> 38:14.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:14.956 --> 38:17.696 align:middle
So we don't-you know, we-the
dollar is really the business

38:17.696 --> 38:18.966 align:middle
of the Treasury Department.

38:18.966 --> 38:22.486 align:middle
It's certainly a financial
condition unto itself that plays

38:22.486 --> 38:26.146 align:middle
into our models, but we
don't, you know, seek or model

38:26.196 --> 38:29.776 align:middle
or attempt to affect the dollar
directly with our policies.

38:31.046 --> 38:32.816 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

38:34.476 --> 38:38.996 align:middle
Hi. John Heltman-John
Heltman with American Banker.

38:39.346 --> 38:43.256 align:middle
I have a question about the
Fed's proposal from October

38:43.256 --> 38:46.226 align:middle
of last year regarding
enhanced prudential standards

38:46.226 --> 38:49.826 align:middle
for banks above $250 billion.

38:50.136 --> 38:51.626 align:middle
Some analysts have suggested

38:51.626 --> 38:55.686 align:middle
that that proposal could
encourage consolidation mergers

38:55.686 --> 38:57.856 align:middle
and acquisition among
banks, particularly

38:57.856 --> 38:59.816 align:middle
at the upper end of the range.

39:00.116 --> 39:05.026 align:middle
I'm curious if the Fed
anticipated that outcome

39:05.336 --> 39:07.866 align:middle
and whether you have
any reservations

39:07.866 --> 39:09.756 align:middle
or whether you-the Fed cares

39:09.756 --> 39:12.656 align:middle
about bank consolidation
more generally.

39:13.546 --> 39:13.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:13.946 --> 39:16.546 align:middle
We're not motivated
by a particular view

39:16.696 --> 39:19.676 align:middle
of industry structure that
we're trying to achieve

39:19.726 --> 39:20.696 align:middle
through our regulation.

39:21.176 --> 39:23.626 align:middle
I think we want to have
banks of all different sizes

39:23.626 --> 39:27.116 align:middle
and with different business
models out there carrying

39:27.116 --> 39:28.366 align:middle
out their functions
in the economy.

39:28.646 --> 39:30.456 align:middle
So that's how we
think about that.

39:30.456 --> 39:34.076 align:middle
I would also say, when
banks merge and move

39:34.076 --> 39:37.766 align:middle
into a larger asset category,
they get stronger regulation,

39:37.766 --> 39:38.696 align:middle
not weaker regulation.

39:39.486 --> 39:41.976 align:middle
So if you think about
it, you know,

39:42.066 --> 39:44.246 align:middle
if you're a medium-sized
bank and you merge

39:44.246 --> 39:46.856 align:middle
with another medium-sized
bank, you wind up being more

39:46.856 --> 39:48.606 align:middle
than a medium-sized
bank, you know.

39:48.606 --> 39:53.546 align:middle
But the sense of our
tapering is that-"tailoring,"

39:53.546 --> 39:55.786 align:middle
sorry-our tailoring policy is

39:55.786 --> 39:59.116 align:middle
that the highest expectations
fall upon the largest,

39:59.486 --> 40:03.326 align:middle
most systemically important,
most complex institutions,

40:03.326 --> 40:05.216 align:middle
and then at every
step along the way,

40:06.136 --> 40:09.016 align:middle
the expectations become
more tailored to the risks

40:09.016 --> 40:12.636 align:middle
that that institution actually
presents to the economy.

40:12.636 --> 40:15.656 align:middle
So- BRIAN CHEUNG.

40:16.776 --> 40:20.626 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

40:20.626 --> 40:21.726 align:middle
Brian Cheung with Yahoo Finance.

40:21.726 --> 40:22.956 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

40:22.956 --> 40:24.896 align:middle
So as the Fed remains on pause,

40:24.936 --> 40:27.906 align:middle
does the central bank find
the onus on fiscal policy

40:27.906 --> 40:31.446 align:middle
to extend its economic recovery
and fend off a slowdown-as in,

40:31.446 --> 40:34.386 align:middle
is the Fed positioning itself
to take on a reactionary role

40:34.386 --> 40:36.706 align:middle
to whatever fiscal
policy is doing?

40:36.976 --> 40:40.256 align:middle
I'm asking because we're seeing
the waning effect of tax reform

40:40.256 --> 40:42.146 align:middle
and no material news
on infrastructure,

40:42.146 --> 40:42.916 align:middle
so just wondering kind

40:42.916 --> 40:46.086 align:middle
of how you feel the monetary
policy-fiscal policy balance is

40:46.086 --> 40:46.746 align:middle
in swing right now.

40:47.256 --> 40:47.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:47.816 --> 40:52.116 align:middle
So we take-we take fiscal
policy as, you know,

40:52.116 --> 40:53.986 align:middle
as exogenous to what we do.

40:53.986 --> 40:56.216 align:middle
You know, in other
words, whatever happens

40:56.216 --> 40:58.976 align:middle
with fiscal policy,
we take that.

40:58.976 --> 41:01.406 align:middle
We don't evaluate it,
we don't criticize it,

41:01.506 --> 41:03.356 align:middle
and we don't overreact
to it either.

41:03.356 --> 41:05.696 align:middle
So I think it's just a fact.

41:05.696 --> 41:08.246 align:middle
It's just an external fact
about the economy for us.

41:08.796 --> 41:11.866 align:middle
I mean, our policy is-the
reason we're on hold is

41:11.866 --> 41:13.746 align:middle
that we think our policy
rate is in a good place,

41:14.356 --> 41:16.286 align:middle
and we think the economy
is in a good place,

41:16.366 --> 41:20.256 align:middle
and we're watching carefully
as we see these events evolve

41:20.256 --> 41:21.366 align:middle
around the world and at home,

41:21.426 --> 41:26.736 align:middle
and we think that's what
we need to be doing.

41:27.026 --> 41:28.216 align:middle
STEVEN BECKNER.

41:28.396 --> 41:31.186 align:middle
Steve Beckner, freelance
journalist reporting

41:31.186 --> 41:32.516 align:middle
for NPR, Mr. Chairman.

41:33.116 --> 41:36.716 align:middle
The Fed has been allowing the
average maturity of securities

41:36.716 --> 41:39.506 align:middle
in its bond portfolio
to lengthen.

41:40.036 --> 41:42.816 align:middle
Is this aimed at-consciously
aimed

41:42.816 --> 41:46.976 align:middle
at flattening the yield curve,
and is that going to be part

41:46.976 --> 41:49.596 align:middle
of the Fed's longer-run
balance sheet strategy?

41:50.036 --> 41:54.576 align:middle
And if flattening the yield
curve is a conscious long-term

41:54.576 --> 41:59.686 align:middle
strategy, are you concerned
about the side effect

41:59.686 --> 42:03.996 align:middle
of the heightening
concerns about a flatter

42:03.996 --> 42:06.686 align:middle
or inverted yield curve being
a harbinger of recession?

42:07.346 --> 42:07.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:07.556 --> 42:10.116 align:middle
The basic answer to
your question is "no."

42:10.116 --> 42:15.066 align:middle
We are-the decision about
the maturity composition

42:15.066 --> 42:17.886 align:middle
of the Fed's balance sheet in
the longer run lies ahead of us.

42:18.446 --> 42:19.666 align:middle
We have not made that decision,

42:19.666 --> 42:24.176 align:middle
and we're-we really haven't
begun to have a serious series

42:24.176 --> 42:27.836 align:middle
of discussions over a series of
meetings to grapple with that.

42:27.836 --> 42:30.966 align:middle
That, I think, will be something
we turn to reasonably soon,

42:30.966 --> 42:33.116 align:middle
but I think it will
take some time.

42:33.116 --> 42:37.356 align:middle
It's a consequential decision
and one that needs some thought.

42:37.386 --> 42:40.176 align:middle
We've had a lot of balance sheet
discussions, as I mentioned,

42:40.176 --> 42:41.356 align:middle
over the last four meetings,

42:41.356 --> 42:44.276 align:middle
but this is the next big
one that-big decision

42:44.276 --> 42:44.886 align:middle
that we'll face.

42:44.916 --> 42:46.416 align:middle
And, you know, I think we're
not going to be in a rush

42:46.416 --> 42:49.026 align:middle
to resolve it, but
we'll turn to it soon.

42:49.026 --> 42:49.536 align:middle
STEVEN BECKNER.

42:49.536 --> 42:50.656 align:middle
Can I just follow up, please?

42:51.036 --> 42:53.366 align:middle
Well, how do you
account for the fact

42:53.416 --> 42:56.896 align:middle
that the average maturity
has in fact been lengthening

42:56.896 --> 42:59.256 align:middle
and contributing to a
flattening of the yield curve?

42:59.256 --> 43:01.876 align:middle
Is that-what's-what's
causing that?

43:01.876 --> 43:02.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:02.346 --> 43:06.536 align:middle
I think, isn't it just that
as securities roll off,

43:07.066 --> 43:10.046 align:middle
you wind up investing in-you
know, as a 10-year rolls off,

43:10.046 --> 43:12.486 align:middle
you wind up investing in a
10-year, and automatically

43:12.486 --> 43:13.286 align:middle
that lengthens things.

43:13.506 --> 43:16.546 align:middle
It's not at all a plan to
lengthen the balance sheet.

43:16.546 --> 43:18.716 align:middle
It's just been-we try
to have a practice

43:18.796 --> 43:21.076 align:middle
that we don't deviate
from, and it's transparent,

43:21.886 --> 43:27.666 align:middle
and-no policy message in that.

43:28.006 --> 43:28.476 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

43:31.286 --> 43:32.886 align:middle
Hi, Jean Yung with Market News.

43:33.096 --> 43:36.916 align:middle
I wanted to ask, at what
point do you expect to begin

43:36.916 --> 43:39.636 align:middle
to allow the balance sheet
to grow slowly again?

43:40.096 --> 43:41.736 align:middle
What are-how will you
make that decision?

43:42.456 --> 43:43.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:43.456 --> 43:47.046 align:middle
So, as I-as I mentioned, the
balance sheet runoff will stop

43:47.396 --> 43:49.766 align:middle
at about-at September 30.

43:49.766 --> 43:54.366 align:middle
And if it is our
view at that time

43:54.586 --> 43:58.256 align:middle
that we're still a
ways away from-a ways

43:58.256 --> 44:03.306 align:middle
above a balance sheet that is
what we need to efficiently

44:03.306 --> 44:04.936 align:middle
and effectively conduct
monetary policy,

44:05.426 --> 44:08.096 align:middle
at that time we will hold
the balance sheet constant.

44:08.406 --> 44:12.036 align:middle
And then what'll happen is,
organically, very gradually,

44:12.716 --> 44:15.156 align:middle
currency and other nonreserved
liabilities will grow,

44:15.156 --> 44:16.186 align:middle
and reserves will shrink.

44:16.916 --> 44:19.616 align:middle
And the question you're asking
is, how long will that go on?

44:19.726 --> 44:22.436 align:middle
The truth is, we don't know,
and we don't really know

44:22.436 --> 44:24.516 align:middle
that we'll move past
September 30th.

44:24.936 --> 44:30.176 align:middle
The level of reserve demand is
something that we've put a lot

44:30.176 --> 44:33.036 align:middle
of effort and time into
creating estimates,

44:33.076 --> 44:35.456 align:middle
based on market intelligence and
surveys and that kind of thing.

44:35.456 --> 44:36.586 align:middle
The truth is, we don't know.

44:36.906 --> 44:38.166 align:middle
It may evolve over time.

44:38.256 --> 44:40.626 align:middle
So we'll just have to see.

44:40.756 --> 44:43.076 align:middle
I wouldn't want to put a
time out there for that,

44:43.676 --> 44:47.666 align:middle
but-so I'll just leave it there.

44:47.786 --> 44:51.566 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

44:53.106 --> 44:54.596 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

44:54.596 --> 44:55.696 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

44:55.866 --> 45:00.146 align:middle
Are you concerned at all what
the market impact will be should

45:00.146 --> 45:03.316 align:middle
the Fed resume policy tightening
somewhere down the line?

45:03.426 --> 45:04.806 align:middle
As I'm sure you know,
the market listens

45:04.806 --> 45:06.146 align:middle
to you very, very closely.

45:06.886 --> 45:07.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:08.396 --> 45:13.586 align:middle
You know, I'm just going
to say that I think we're

45:13.586 --> 45:15.996 align:middle
in a good place right now,
which is, we're being patient,

45:15.996 --> 45:20.476 align:middle
we're watching, we don't see any
data pushing us to move rates

45:20.476 --> 45:24.726 align:middle
in either direction, and
we're going to watch carefully

45:24.726 --> 45:29.356 align:middle
and patiently as we
allow events to evolve.

45:29.606 --> 45:38.426 align:middle
And when they do clarify,
we will act appropriately.

